// `Promise.all()` is risky because it fails fast: when any promise rejects,
// the aggregate promise immediately rejects, discarding access to the other
// results. However, the remaining operations continue executing, potentially
// causing side effects or failures after the caller has already moved on.
//
// `Promise.allSettled()` is generally a safer default for independent
// concurrent operations because it:
//
// - Waits for every promise to settle, whether fulfilled or rejected.
// - Preserves each operation's outcome, allowing explicit error handling.
// - Supports partial success and prevents one failure from obscuring other results.
//
// Recommendation: Prefer `Promise.allSettled()` for independent operations,
// especially those with side effects. Use `Promise.all()` when all results are
// required and failing fast is intentional.
//
// Important: Neither method cancels outstanding operations or provides
// transactional atomicity. `allSettled()` also requires callers to inspect and
// handle rejected results explicitly.

language js

`Promise.all($args)` as $call where {
  register_diagnostic(
    span = $call,
    message = "Promise.all discards completed work when any item rejects. Use Promise.allSettled and report the failure margin. If this is a fixed tuple where every value is required, suppress it with `// biome-ignore lint/plugin/no-promise-all: <reason>`.",
    severity = "error"
  )
}
